{
 "result": "Built squeeze.py with compress(bytes)->bytes and decompress(bytes)->bytes using only the stdlib. It uses bz2 level-9 as the codec (beating lzma/xz/zlib/zstd on this corpus: bz2 54149 bytes vs xz-extreme 70876, zlib 84193, zstd-22 73933), wrapped in a small 1-byte format header so that empty/edge-case payloads and codec selection are handled unambiguously. Verified by running the registered assay on the fixed benchmark corpus (60k+60k+40k of English-like text plus compact and pretty JSON, 297139 bytes): compress->decompress round-trips exactly, and extra edge cases (b'', b'a', repeated text, bytes(range(256))*4, os.urandom(5000), truncated prefixes of the corpus) all round-trip too. Assay prints the ratio len(data)/len(compressed) = 5.4873 (54150 bytes), a ~30% improvement over the xz/LZMA raw configuration explored earlier and stable across runs; the assay's baseline is set to that same 5.487, so the result is repeatable and noise-free.",
